                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Juneau AK",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 15 at 9:16AM AKDT until October 16 at 1:00PM AKDT by NWS Juneau AK",
                "description": "A stronger atmospheric river continues to move over the panhandle\nThursday, lasting through Friday morning. Multiple waves moving up\nwill bring persistent moderate to heavy rain for much of the southern\nhalf of the panhandle, with the heaviest rain expected late morning\nthrough the evening hours Thursday. Elevated gusty winds are expected\nalongside this heavier rainfall, with the strongest winds expected\nThursday night. Prolonged periods of moderate to heavy rain will\nproduce significant river rises with the potential for at least minor\nflooding. Gusty winds alongside the long duration heavy rainfall will\nbring a possibility of isolated landslides.\n\n* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The following area, Southern Inner Channels. This includes\nthe cities of Ketchikan And Metlakatla.\n\n* WHEN...From 1 PM AKDT this afternoon through Friday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, other low-lying areas and flood-prone locations.\nStreet flooding and flooding of creeks and rivers are possible.\nIsolated landslides are possible in areas of steep terrain.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Rain totals between early Thursday morning and late Friday\nmorning for the Ketchikan and Metlakatla area are still expected\nto be around 4 to 6 inches, with higher rain totals favoring\nsouthern facing mountain sides. Highest rain rates are currently\nexpected during the late morning through evening timeframe on\nThursday, where rates could exceed 0.3 inches per hour.\n\nConfidence has increased for the area seeing winds of 25 to 35\nmph with gusts to 55 mph on Thursday at sea level, and exceeding\n60 mph at elevation, with the highest winds expected Thursday\nevening into the night. With the multiple rounds of moderate to\nheavy rainfall and the elevated winds Thursday, there is\npossibility for isolated landslides along steep mountain slopes.",
                "instruction": "Stay tuned to further developments by listening to your local radio,\ntelevision, or NOAA Weather Radio for further information.\n\nYou should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.",
